WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Type III covers low-pressure chillers. These are commercial systems — big
equipment, big consequences.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Type III covers low-pressure systems primarily centrifugal chillers used in
large commercial buildings. These systems use refrigerants like R-11, R-123, and
R-245fa that boil at temperatures above typical conditions at atmospheric

00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Low-Pressure System: Operates below atmospheric pressure. Air leaks in rather
than refrigerant leaking out.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Centrifugal Chiller: Large commercial cooling system using a centrifugal
compressor and low-pressure refrigerant.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Here's why this matters on the job: Type III is less common for residential
technicians but required for Universal certification and commercial building
work.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Watch out for these: Low-pressure systems operate in a vacuum. And — Air leaks
IN not refrigerant leaks OUT

00:02:37.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Why does a low-pressure system have air leaking in rather than refrigerant
leaking out? Think about the pressure relationship.

